## 8. Rule Management

This is the application's core feature.

Users must be able to create, inspect, edit, duplicate, enable, disable, reorder, and delete forwarding rules.

Each rule must support:

- Unique identifier.
- User-defined name.
- Description, if useful.
- Enabled/disabled status.
- One source chat.
- One or more destination chats.
- Priority from 0 to 100.
- Exclusive-stop behavior.
- Message-type filter.
- Sender filter.
- Required keywords.
- Excluded keywords.
- Regular-expression filters.
- Case sensitivity.
- Whole-word matching.
- Minimum and maximum file size.
- Filename substring matching.
- Filename suffix matching.
- Filename regular expression.
- Automatic content categories.
- ALL-versus-ANY category matching.
- Schedule.
- Forward or copy mode.
- Caption transformation settings.
- Per-rule delay.
- Retry configuration.
- Edit/delete synchronization options.

Create an intuitive rule editor divided into logical sections or tabs:

1. General.
2. Source and destinations.
3. Message and sender filters.
4. Keywords and regular expressions.
5. File filters.
6. Media categories.
7. Scheduling.
8. Delivery settings.
9. Caption transformations.
10. Advanced options.

Show validation errors inline and preserve unsaved form state when appropriate.

### Rule priority

Evaluate rules in descending priority order, with a deterministic secondary ordering for equal priorities.

When Exclusive stop is enabled, stop evaluating lower-priority rules only after the current rule has passed all its filters and is considered a match.

Make the exact meaning of a match explicit and consistent. A rule that does not match must never block lower-priority rules.

If multiple matching rules target the same destination, use the configured duplicate policy.

### Rule testing

Provide a Test Rule feature that evaluates the rule against a selected historical message or a representative message supplied by the user.

Display:

- Whether the rule matched.
- Which conditions passed.
- Which conditions failed.
- Which category was assigned.
- Which destinations would receive the message.
- Whether exclusive-stop logic would apply.
- Any transformations that would be applied.

Testing must never send a message unless the user explicitly chooses a separate, clearly labeled live-delivery test action.

